RAWRXD_RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_VERDICT_001
======================================
THE COMPARISON THAT WAS NEVER MADE, NOW RUN.
OVERLAY vs THE BEST SINGLE-FORMAT QUANTISER AT MATCHED BITRATE.

TOOL   rawrxd/certs/gguf_tensor_slice_001.cpp  (stage 4)
METHOD RTN min/max quantisers at the EXACT bits-per-weight of each shipped
       format (bits + 16/groupSize), scored against the SAME fixed probe x as
       the overlay points, so the two curves are directly comparable.
       All 4 cases: CHECKS_FAIL=0, FINAL_VERDICT=PASS


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. RESULT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
tensor                    overlay_bpw  overlay_L2 | best_direct<=overlay   direct_L2
                                                              premium_bpw
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MoE blk.20.ffn_down_exps      7.2993    0.000000 |  7.0000  0.017120     +0.30
MoE blk.0.ffn_gate_exps       7.2568    0.000000 |  7.0000  0.016852     +0.26
dense blk.31.attn_q          11.7107    0.000000 |  8.5000  0.002356     +3.21
dense blk.63.ffn_down        13.4188    0.000000 |  9.0000  0.003559     +4.42

  OVERLAY_IS_DOMINATED_AT_THAT_FIDELITY = 1   in ALL FOUR cases

AND at a conventional quality bar rather than exactness:

  tensor                    cheapest_direct_reaching_L2<=0.05   overlay_bpw
  MoE IQ3_XXS                                 6.0000                 7.2993
  MoE IQ2_XS                                  5.0000                 7.2568
  dense Q4_K                                  4.5000                11.7107
  dense Q6_K                                  5.2500                13.4188

A SINGLE quantiser reaches rel_L2 <= 0.05 for 4.5-6.0 bits/element, while the
overlay spends 7.3-13.4 to reach exactly zero error. On the dense tensors the
overlay costs 2.6x more than a rival that already clears the bar.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE BASELINE WAS BIASED AGAINST THIS CONCLUSION, AND IT STILL HELD
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTN is a GENEROUS baseline. Production k-quants and i-quants beat RTN at the
same rate -- they use better scale placement, outlier handling and (for i-quants)
learned codebooks. So:

  * losing to RTN   means losing to the real shipped formats as well;
  * beating RTN     would have left a HARDER test, not an easier one.

The comparison was therefore constructed to favour the overlay, and the overlay
lost anyway. Every other number in this investigation had a defensible
alternative reading; this one does not.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHY IT LOSES -- THE MECHANISM, NOT JUST THE SCORE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The overlay buys exactness. That is its only real advantage, and it is bought
with three costs that do not appear in a bits-per-weight column:

  1. A SECOND storage tier. The delta is not co-resident with the base; it is
     fetched, which is the entire premise of the architecture.
  2. A SECOND decode pass, per layer, on the hot path.
  3. A FETCH whose latency is paid during forward execution -- the exact cost the
     Direct-I/O residual overlay was designed to hide, and which measurement
     shows is not hidden, only relocated.

And what it buys for that: exactness from rel_L2 0.017 to 0.000, at a fidelity
level a single format already reaches within its own budget. The overlay is
buying a difference that does not matter, with costs that do.

For the dense tensors the premium is 3.2-4.4 bpw -- larger than the entire
base. That is not a marginal loss; it is the wrong shape.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. A SECONDARY FINDING: RTN DEGRADES AT FINER GROUP SIZES
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Not every row is monotone in bits, and the reason is worth recording:

  dense blk.31.attn_q   4 bits/group32 = 4.50 bpw -> rel_L2 0.044853
                        4 bits/group16 = 5.00 bpw -> rel_L2 0.059944

MORE bits produced a WORSE reconstruction. That is not a bug in the harness; it
is the classic RTN failure on outlier-heavy weight tensors. With a 16-element
group, one outlier consumes most of the group's dynamic range and the other 15
values are crushed into few levels. Halving the group size increases scale
overhead AND increases outlier sensitivity, so it can lose.

The consequence for this receipt is that the reported direct figures are the
BEST over all configurations, which is the conservative choice for the overlay.
Any single-format number chosen by an author rather than by a min-over-configs
would be worse, and would flatter the overlay further.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. FINAL VERDICT ON THE RESIDUAL OVERLAY
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LOW_RANK_RESIDUAL_VARIANT   REFUTED PROVABLY
      rank(W) <= rank(base) + rank(delta) puts the floor for any two-factor
      split at 36.9-38.6 bits/element for a 32-bit original. Measured on real
      tensors, 92-96% full rank. Not a tuning problem; arithmetic.

  RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_GENERAL     REFUTED ON THE MERITS
      7.3-13.4 bpw for exactness, against 4.5-6.0 bpw from a single format to
      reach rel_L2 <= 0.05. Dominated on bits at every fidelity tested, and
      carrying a second tier, a second decode and a hot-path fetch on top.

  DELTA_STRUCTURE              NONE FOUND
      not sparse (2-13% near-zero), not low-rank (rank 188-248 of 256), not
      low-entropy (3.5-7.2 bits/element, and that IS the floor).

  The premise -- "split the weights so the correction becomes cheap" -- is
  false for these tensors. The correction is neither cheap nor structured, and
  the direct-quantisation path that already exists in every shipped format is
  strictly better on the only axis that was measured.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHAT WOULD CHANGE THIS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The overlay fails because the delta is expensive and unstructured. A delta
becomes cheap only if the BASE is chosen so its error is small AND structured.
That is a different, testable proposal -- and it has a sharp formulation:

      minimise  bits(base) + H(residual)   subject to  residual low-rank

rather than minimising base bits alone. The harness now measures both terms
plus the exact rank curve, so that optimisation is runnable without new
instrumentation.

It is worth attempting ONLY if the win would exceed the 3.2-4.4 bpw the
structureless version already gives away. That is a high bar, and the honest
starting position is that the direct-format path has not been beaten.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. LIMITS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Direct figures are RTN, not llama.cpp's tuned k-quants. This FAVOURS the
    overlay; a tuned Q4_K/Q5_K would push the direct numbers lower still.
  * rel_L2 is measured against a single fixed Gaussian probe vector per tensor.
    Two points on one probe; a matrix of probes would tighten it.
  * Windows are 256x1024; layer coverage 0/20/31/63, not exhaustive.
  * Point B has rel_L2 = 0.000000 BY CONSTRUCTION, since the delta is defined
    as W - base. That is the overlay's definition of correctness, not a
    measurement of reconstruction quality; the direct figures at the same
    bitrate are the meaningful comparison and they are the ones used here.
  * No engine, GPU, I/O or residency linkage. This is arithmetic on real weights
    read directly out of real GGUF files.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOREBOARD, WHOLE INVESTIGATION
  synthetic 75-config sweep        cross-validated against real weights
  dense Q4_K / Q6_K                11 cases, low-rank 0/11
  MoE IQ2_XS / IQ3_XXS / MXFP4     10 cases, low-rank 0/10
  rank bound                       floor 36.9-38.6 bits > 32-bit original
  matched-bitrate comparison       OVERLAY DOMINATED 4/4
  RESIDUAL_OVERLAY                 NOT VIABLE on the evidence available